<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berkyaruq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1081">1081</a>–1105</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berkyaruq was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Great Seljuk Empire</a> from 1094 to 1105, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a>. His reign was taken up by wars with his uncle Tutush and his brothers, above all Muhammad Tapar, which divided the empire. These conflicts left the Seljuks unable to respond effectively to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berkyaruq.jpg" alt="Berkyaruq" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid al-Din Hamadani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BarkiyaruqPainting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Berkyaruq, whose name is a Turkic word meaning &quot;firm, unwavering light&quot;, was the oldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a> and the Seljuk princess Zubayda Khatun, and was born at the Seljuk capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. When Malik-Shah died in November 1092 no prince was old enough to inherit the empire, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> and court figures each backed one of his young sons. Malik-Shah's wife Terken Khatun and the vizier Taj al-Mulk installed her four-year-old son Mahmud at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mustazhir over, while the family of the dead vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyya</a>, smuggled Berkyaruq out of Isfahan, and he was crowned at Ray.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In January 1093 Berkyaruq's supporters defeated Mahmud's and captured Taj al-Mulk, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizamiyya">Nizamiyya</a> had executed on 12 February. Terken Khatun and Mahmud both died in 1094. Berkyaruq's uncle Tutush then invaded western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and seized Ray, but Berkyaruq's forces killed him near the city on 25 February 1095, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> acknowledged Berkyaruq as sultan. In the east, his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a> was made ruler of Khurasan after an earlier army failed, and Berkyaruq confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Qarakhanids</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and appointed Qutb al-Din Muhammad governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, which began the Khwarazmian dynasty. Revolts then spread through his eastern lands.</p><p>The gravest challenge was the rebellion of his half-brother Muhammad Tapar in 1098 or 1099, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>'s son Mu'ayyid al-Mulk. The five-year war was indecisive, and by 1104, with his treasury exhausted, Berkyaruq made a treaty that left him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Preoccupied with these conflicts, he did not respond to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 1097. He died of tuberculosis in 1105 on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, and his infant son Malik-Shah II succeeded him. Muhammad then captured Baghdad and had the child killed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berkyaruq” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tutush was killed in battle near Rayy in 1095 fighting his nephew <b>Berkyaruq</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-syria">Seljuk Sultanate of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Borujerd, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1096" aria-label="Isfahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Berkyaruq"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4403 4751h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4403 4751h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.65,51.68&amp;year=1096">What was here in 1096? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
